<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>eu.wohlben.qits</groupId>
        <artifactId>qits-workspaces</artifactId>
        <version>2026.905.164818</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <!-- The workspaces SERVICE layer: the technical half. JAX-RS route definitions, the SSE
         broadcast channels, and the workspace-daemon control socket + registry — everything that
         needs a web stack and therefore cannot live in `domain`.

         A DEPLOYABLE APP, not a library JAR. It was extracted as a library — "a consuming Quarkus
         application pulls it in and gets the routes" — because packaging it would have needed an
         auth variant, a webui and a main class. All three reasons have lapsed: authentication
         terminates at qits-gateway and this module reads a header (see security/), the webui stays
         in the monorepo, and Quarkus supplies the main class.

         This was the last of the six extracted services to be packaged, because it is the only one
         with a MANDATORY port: three @Inject RepositoryLookup sites took augmentation down until
         wiring/UnconfiguredRepositoryLookup gave them a @DefaultBean to resolve against. That bean
         then refuses to start a production build, which is the behaviour the port's javadoc
         specifies. Building and running are not the same gate.

         See migration-deployables-plan.md in the superproject. -->
    <artifactId>qits-workspaces-service</artifactId>
    <name>qits-workspaces :: service</name>
    <packaging>quarkus</packaging>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <!-- /<segment>/q/health/ready - the readiness endpoint qits-cd's health gate curls;
                 the deployment convention every service answers. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-smallrye-health</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-workspaces-domain</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The daemon wire contract. Vendored into this repo (see that module's pom) because
                 it is published to no registry. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-workspaces-daemon-protocol</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The platform event bus client. Joining it here is what turns a library module into
                 a deployable that publishes: the outbox's datasource, its Flyway lineage and the
                 sweeper's scheduled job all arrive with this jar, configured by the defaults it
                 ships in its own META-INF/microprofile-config.properties. The only things this
                 module says about it are where it is dark (application.properties) and where its
                 database goes in a container with no home directory (docker/Dockerfile).

                 It brings quarkus-scheduler, which is NEW to this deployable and the only extension
                 this dependency adds that was not already here. quarkus-undertow stays absent, as
                 Quinoa requires:
                 `./mvnw -pl service -am dependency:tree -Dincludes=io.quarkus:quarkus-undertow`
                 is still empty. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-eventstream</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- DbRetry, used by name in ContainerProxyRoute's daemon lookup. It also arrives
                 through `domain`, which names PatientPgDriver in the datasource block it ships;
                 declared here as well because this module calls the class. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-db-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The orchestrator's client: every container and volume this service has goes through
                 it, and there is no docker vocabulary left anywhere else. A plain jar over
                 java.net.http — no arc, no OIDC extension, no framework — so the bean and the token
                 are this module's to supply (containers/ContainersClientProducer).

                 It is platform infrastructure rather than another bounded context: no domain model
                 crosses it, every call is bounded and cannot throw, and qits-containers is the
                 platform's single answer to "where do containers live", the way qits-events is to
                 "where do events live".

                 A native consumer owes the wire records a registration — the jar builds its own
                 ObjectMapper, so nothing scans them: containers/ContainersWireReflection, the
                 sibling of bus/EventWireReflection and there for the identical reason. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-containers-client</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The machine token this service presents to qits-containers, which guards every route
                 on the caller's own identity. quarkus.oidc-client.client-id is therefore not a label
                 here — it is this service's OWNER string, and qits.workspace.containers.owner
                 defaults to reading it. Shipped disabled; see application.properties. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-oidc-client</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- A workspace daemon dials this service directly on qits-net. Its control WebSocket
                 requires qits:system, so the commissioned bearer must become a validated
                 SecurityIdentity here rather than relying on browser forward-auth at the edge. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-oidc</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-arc</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- OTLP export to qits-observability. The extension is what instruments the process;
                 the endpoint and protocol in application.properties are what make it arrive, since
                 the defaults are gRPC to localhost:4317 and the receiver speaks HTTP/protobuf. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-opentelemetry</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- SecurityIdentity + the HttpAuthenticationMechanism SPI, for the single thing this
                 service does about auth: read qits-gateway's X-Qits-User header into an identity
                 (see security/ForwardAuthMechanism). NOT an auth variant and NOT an authorization
                 policy — the monolith's -Dqits.variant auth/* modules do not come here at all;
                 authentication terminates at the gateway (migration-auth-plan.md). -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-auth-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-rest-jackson</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The OUTBOUND half: wiring/ProjectsRepositories, the one call this service makes to
                 another — asking qits-projects about a repository, because this context holds no
                 foreign key into the repositories tables and there is no join to make across two
                 physical databases.

                 The declarative client rather than a hand-rolled java.net.http one, for a reason
                 that is about the topology and not about taste: `quarkus.rest-client.*.url` accepts
                 `stork://qits-projects`, so moving from a fixed address to real service discovery
                 (Consul, Kubernetes, a static list) is a config change instead of a rewrite. It
                 also puts the timeouts in config and the 404-vs-error split in one place. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-rest-client-jackson</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The daemon control socket (@WebSocket .../{workspaceId}). -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-websockets-next</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- Multi/BroadcastProcessor: the per-workspace SSE fan-out. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-mutiny</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-hibernate-validator</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- In-process reverse proxy for a service's web view (/services/* -> the container's
                 published port; ServiceProxyRoute). Managed by the platform BOM, so no version
                 here; forwards WebSocket upgrades (dev-server HMR) by default. -->
            <groupId>io.vertx</groupId>
            <artifactId>vertx-http-proxy</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-smallrye-openapi</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The SPA at /workspaces. Quinoa runs the Angular build in src/main/webui (a submodule,
                 qits-spa-workspaces) during augmentation and serves the output from inside the
                 artifact, so the segment this service already owns carries the UI as well as the API
                 and there is no second process to deploy.

                 Note what must NOT join it: quarkus-undertow breaks Quinoa's production static
                 serving (qits-artifacts' service/pom.xml records the same rule, and the monorepo's
                 docs/issues/2026-07-15_packaged-spa-not-served.md is the incident). Nothing here
                 pulls it in today — the reverse proxies are vertx-http-proxy and the raw routes are
                 Vert.x — and nothing may start to; a servlet container arriving transitively would
                 show up as a blank page from the packaged artifact with a green suite behind it,
                 because Quinoa is disabled by default in tests and the suite never serves the SPA at
                 all. Version from the root pom: quarkiverse, not quarkus-bom. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkiverse.quinoa</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-quinoa</artifactId>
            <version>${quinoa.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-junit</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.rest-assured</groupId>
            <artifactId>rest-assured</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- DatasourceBaselineRules, asserted by DatasourceBaselineTest. It runs HERE and not
                 in `domain` because this is the module whose config is the whole config the
                 deployable runs on: both postgresql datasources, `workspaces` from the domain jar
                 and `eventstream` from the eventstream jar. `domain`'s own ArchRulesTest uses the
                 same jar for the entity rules. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-arch-rules</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The user-story framework: this module's story catalogue is written as @UserStory
                 methods, so the packaged-boot proof doubles as documentation — steps, notes and an
                 observed network diagram emitted under target/userstories/. Every story is
                 browserless (they take Interactions and no Flow), so the transitive Playwright
                 never launches anything and no Chromium is needed to build this module. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-userflows</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The recording service mocks. The IT points quarkus.oidc at idp.MockIdp — a
                 MockService plus key material: it serves the JWKS for a generated keypair, mints
                 tokens signed by it (and, on demand, by a key it never published), and records what
                 it answered, which is the far end of the story's interactions. It is the one thing
                 DaemonControlSocketMachineAuthTest cannot be: that test hands Quarkus a static
                 quarkus.oidc.public-key, so the shipped auth-server-url + jwks-path pair — a real
                 fetch over a real listener at startup — is exercised nowhere else in this repo. -->
            <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
            <artifactId>qits-service-mock</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The store every @QuarkusTest here boots against — BOTH of this module's datasources,
                 `workspaces` and the qits-eventstream jar's `eventstream`. Zonky spawns real
                 postgres binaries as a child process, a maven dependency rather than a container,
                 which is what keeps the clone-alone, docker-free rule true now that both stores are
                 postgres. EmbeddedPg + EmbeddedPgConfigSource hand the running instance to Quarkus.
                 The copy in `domain` is deliberate: the two modules share no test classpath. -->
            <groupId>io.zonky.test</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- Named per architecture on purpose: the binaries are separate artifacts and zonky
                 picks one at run time, so an unnamed arch fails in the middle of a suite rather than
                 during resolution. These three are what this platform is built and developed on. -->
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-linux-amd64</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-linux-arm64v8</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The fourth arch, and it is CI's. .config/qits/ci-event-userflows.yml runs
                 `mvn verify` in maven-base as `user: build`, and those step containers are Alpine
                 (musl): zonky picks the -alpine binaries there at run time, and
                 TokenValidationBootstrapIT's own embedded postgres is spawned by this module's
                 failsafe JVM. `user: build` for the same family of reason — initdb refuses to run
                 as root and calls getpwuid, so the step needs a passwd-backed non-root user.
                 qits-db-core's pom carries the same fourth arch for the same pipeline. -->
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-linux-amd64-alpine</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-darwin-arm64v8</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <!-- This module is the DEPLOYABLE, not a library jar, and <packaging>quarkus</packaging>
                     above is what says so: it binds generate-code, generate-code-tests and build into
                     the lifecycle, so augmentation is not an execution someone can misplace. It was
                     one here until this commit — and a sibling repo (qits-projects) has already had
                     exactly that execution silently not apply, because its <build> put <testResources>
                     ahead of <plugins>; `mvn verify` stayed green while nothing was augmented, since
                     @QuarkusTest augments on its own regardless of packaging. A packaging type cannot
                     fail that way: drop this <extensions>true</extensions> and the build stops at
                     "Unknown packaging: quarkus". Version comes from the root pom's pluginManagement. -->
                <groupId>${quarkus.platform.group-id}</groupId>
                <artifactId>quarkus-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <extensions>true</extensions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- preferIPv4Stack is for the daemon ITs, and it has to be an argLine because the
                     JVM reads it when networking initialises, before any test runs. Those tests
                     stand a server up in this JVM for a container to dial through
                     host.docker.internal, and Docker Desktop's gateway forwards only IPv4
                     listeners; left alone a JVM binds a dual-stack IPv6 socket for 0.0.0.0, which
                     the container cannot see. Harmless for everything else here — the Quarkus test
                     server is reached over loopback. -->
                <configuration>
                    <argLine>-Djava.net.preferIPv4Stack=true</argLine>
                    <systemPropertyVariables>
                        <java.util.logging.manager>org.jboss.logmanager.LogManager</java.util.logging.manager>
                    </systemPropertyVariables>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>integration-test</goal>
                            <goal>verify</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <systemPropertyVariables>
                                <!-- Where a @QuarkusIntegrationTest would find the compiled binary.
                                     Spelled with quarkus.package.output-name from
                                     application.properties, NOT ${project.build.finalName}-runner:
                                     with an output-name set that path does not exist, so the two
                                     drifting apart means a native IT launches nothing and passes.
                                     No longer inert: TokenValidationBootstrapIT is the
                                     artifact-level IT this line was set in advance for, and a
                                     `-Dnative -DskipITs=false` run launches the binary through
                                     it. -->
                                <native.image.path>${project.build.directory}/qits-workspaces</native.image.path>
                                <!-- Launch the packaged app on a FREE port rather than the default
                                     8081. Two holders of that port, and both are real here: the
                                     last @QuarkusTest instance surefire started can still be
                                     letting go of it when failsafe begins, and on the deployment
                                     host 8081 is where the platform's own npm registry answers.
                                     The test properties set the same value for surefire and say
                                     the same thing at greater length. -->
                                <quarkus.http.test-port>0</quarkus.http.test-port>
                                <java.util.logging.manager>org.jboss.logmanager.LogManager</java.util.logging.manager>
                            </systemPropertyVariables>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>io.smallrye</groupId>
                <artifactId>jandex-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <profiles>
        <!-- GraalVM native build, the same profile qits-workspace-daemon and qits-gateway carry:
             `-Dnative` disables the fast-jar and compiles a binary instead. It lives in THIS module
             and not the root pom because only this module is an application — `domain` and
             `workspace-daemon-protocol` are library jars with nothing to native-compile.

             It deliberately does NOT flip skipITs, which is where this repo differs from its
             siblings. There the native profile turns ITs on so a `-Dnative` build exercises the
             binary it just produced; here five of the six *IT.java are the docker-backed `extended`
             suite (real containers, the published qits/workspace image, the daemon binary) and have
             nothing to say about the artifact — turning them on would make a native build shell
             docker, which is precisely what the clone-alone rule forbids.

             The sixth one IS about the artifact, and it is why this comment no longer ends where it
             used to. TokenValidationBootstrapIT could not be written while the binary refused to
             start without a RepositoryLookup — a @QuarkusIntegrationTest would have failed on the
             documented behaviour rather than on a defect. wiring/HttpRepositoryLookup replaced that
             scaffold, so the address is now something a test profile can simply supply, and it
             does. It is opted in by NAME rather than by this flag:

                 ./mvnw verify -DskipITs=false -Dit.test=TokenValidationBootstrapIT

             and the same two arguments plus `-Dnative` run it against the binary. Naming it is what
             keeps the docker-backed five out of a run that has no business shelling docker. -->

        <profile>
            <id>native</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>native</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <properties>
                <quarkus.package.jar.enabled>false</quarkus.package.jar.enabled>
                <quarkus.native.enabled>true</quarkus.native.enabled>
            </properties>
        </profile>
    </profiles>
</project>
